  ],
  "summary": "Previous research has shown that reliably labeling defensive schemes from NBA data is either untestable due to inferred label noise (FAM:SF-APPA-005) or suffers from extremely low confident label rates (FAM:SF-APPS-058). While the concept of 'suppression' (opponent play impacting next possession PPP) is supported (FAM:SF-APPS-067, FAM:SF-APPA-050), and distinct defensive adaptations can be defined abstractly (FAM:SF-APPM-057), the ability to observe and measure adherence to these defensive strategies in live game play is a major blocker. Adherence to *offensive* recommended mixes has been shown to predict offensive efficiency (FAM:SF-APPM-001), but this does not bridge the gap for defensive strategy measurement. Other related work on mix conformance (FAM:SF-APPS-017) and predictive models (FAM:SF-APPA-020) also did not address the core issue of defensive strategy labeling and adherence."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "The most significant risk. Without reliable, observed labels for specific defensive strategies, it is impossible to define or measure 'adherence to a suppression-conditional recommended mix' on defense. Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPA-005, FAM:SF-APPS-058) strongly indicates this is not feasible with current data and methods."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Even if defensive strategies could be labeled, separating the effect of strategy adherence from other confounding factors (e.g., opponent strength, player skill, random variance, game state, coaching adjustments not related to the 'recommended mix') on winning would be challenging. The 'suppression-conditional' aspect adds another layer of complexity for control variables."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The reliability of the 'suppression-conditional recommended mix' itself. If this mix is derived from a model, its accuracy and stability are critical. Also, how 'adherence' is quantified needs to be reliable and sensitive to actual strategic choices."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Specific 'suppression-conditional' scenarios might be rare, leading to small sample sizes for certain strategy mixes or adherence levels, making it difficult to detect statistically significant effects on winning (FAM:SF-APPS-058, FAM:SF-APPA-050 mention small sample sizes in related contexts)."
  },
  {
   "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
   "text": "If the recommended mix is prescriptive, there might be a 'mechanical floor' for how much a team can deviate due to fundamental basketball rules or player tendencies, making adherence measurement less discriminative (FAM:SF-APPS-017)."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.05,
  "rejected": 0.1,
  "untestable": 0.8,
  "artifact": 0.0,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of a team adhering to a suppression-conditional recommended defensive mix on its probability of winning a game.",
  "unit": "Game-team observation (e.g., Team A in Game 1).",
  "sample": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular season and playoff games where the 'suppression-conditional recommended defensive mix' and observed defensive strategies can be reliably identified and measured. (Note: This sample cannot be reliably constructed due to blocking constraints.)",
  "power": "Not estimable due to fundamental blocking constraints on data/measurement. If testable, power analysis would be conducted based on anticipated effect size and observed variance, likely targeting an effect size on win probability of 0.05-0.10, requiring thousands of game-team observations.",
  "controls": "Control for opponent strength (e.g., using ELO ratings or similar), team strength, game location, game state (score margin, time remaining), player availability, and potentially offensive play-type mix of the opponent. This requires a robust, multivariate regression framework or matching design.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test would involve comparing the effect of 'adherence' to a randomly generated or 'anti-recommended' defensive mix. If the recommended mix still predicts winning, it suggests issues with the model or measurement. Another falsification check would be to see if adherence predicts outcomes for unrelated metrics (e.g., offensive rebounding rate, if not part of the defensive mix).",
  "multiplicity": "If multiple types of suppression-conditional mixes or adherence metrics are tested, appropriate multiplicity corrections (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR) would be applied to p-values.",
  "decision_rule": "A statistically significant (p < 0.05) positive coefficient for defensive adherence on winning probability, robust to various control specifications and sensitivity analyses. The effect size should also be practically meaningful.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, 2025-26 (complete as specified in the task)."
 }
}

